<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.4.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00117</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.4.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8df3b423-e2e2-44f0-bde4-2d635006ea57</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <exercise id="_14dbcfa0-6fce-471d-b730-09851a44d955" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> a solution of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Work smarter, not harder! Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00003">The correct answer is No.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_97304cdc-436a-4375-81ad-81c65c413a95" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00004">Is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> a solution of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">The correct answer is Yes.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a747c5fd-0e3d-455c-abe5-6bde161905d2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00007">Is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> a solution of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">Work smarter, not harder! Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00009">The correct answer is Yes.   You seem to be stuck.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_3a00f8ce-d359-44d8-837b-1e3a937ded6e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">Is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>u</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> a solution of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>u</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>u</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00012">The correct answer is No. You seem to be stuck.Who can you ask for help?</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="b3c93ee1-8ba1-43d0-be55-003676986945" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00013">Tickets to the museum cost $4.50 for children and $9.00 for adults. The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>63</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4.5</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the total cost for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> children’s tickets and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> adult tickets. How many children’s tickets were sold if 4 adult tickets were sold?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00014">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00015">The correct answer is 6.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00016">For questions 6 and 7, use the following scenario: T-shirts are on sale for $12.50. Isla has a coupon for $10.00 off.</para>
      <exercise id="_79fbb396-0500-4dfd-9527-88a0842b3ef1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00017">Choose the equation that represents the total cost, c, if she buys n t-shirts.</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00023">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00024">The correct answer is c = 12.5n-10.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_3cc310a3-9e72-4995-907d-60606fc94d33" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00025">How many T-shirts can she buy if she spends all of her $40?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00026">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00027">The correct answer is 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_9eca9bca-6517-4bb7-b15a-6bc5daf1e385" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00028">An artist is selling children's crafts. Necklaces cost $2.25 each, and bracelets cost $1.50 per each. Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> combinations of necklaces and bracelets that the artist could sell for exactly $12.00</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00029">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00030">The correct answer is: 2 necklaces and 5 bracelets, 4 necklaces and 2 bracelets, No necklaces and 8 bracelets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_55d7874c-c528-49c9-a123-a73eb0d5dd97" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00031">Volunteer drivers are needed to bring 80 students to the championship baseball game. Drivers either have cars, which can seat 4 students, or vans, which can seat 6 students. The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> describes the relationship between the number of cars, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and number of vans, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, that can transport exactly 80 students. Select <emphasis>four</emphasis> statements that are true about the situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00032">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00033">The correct answer is: c=14 and v=4 is a pair of solutions to the equation. If 6 cars go and 11 vans go, there will be extra space. If 20 cars go, no vans are needed. 8 vans and 8 cars are numbers that meet the constraints in this situation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00034">Use the following scenario for questions 11 and 12:</para>
      <para id="para-00035">The drama club is printing T-shirts for its members. The printing company charges a certain amount for each T-shirt plus a setup fee of $40. There are 21 students in the drama club.</para>
      <exercise id="d460c6a6-e642-4be8-b480-b5e6c6985408" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00036">If there are 21 students in the club and the T-shirt order costs a total of $187, how much does each T-shirt cost?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00037">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00038">The correct answer is 7; <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>40</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>21</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>187</m:mn><m:mo>→</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> Each t-shirt costs $7.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_9fddb6dc-98b6-4911-ba3a-d1ee7c8ebe24" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00039">The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>201.50</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6.50</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>21</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> represents the cost of printing the T-shirts at a second printing company. Find the solution to the equation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">
          <para id="para-00040">Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00041">The correct answer is 65; The cost of printing the t-shirts is $65.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>